WEBVTT

00:00:00.917 --> 00:00:08.111
This is Folio Pay Bridge: a small proof of concept of a hotel
payments slice, in Java seventeen with Spring Boot and

00:00:08.111 --> 00:00:15.429
Hibernate. It takes bookings from a property management system
such as Oracle OPERA, sends payment requests, charges a

00:00:15.429 --> 00:00:22.808
virtual card, and posts the payment to the guest folio. The
processor and the PMS are fakes, the data is synthetic, and

00:00:22.808 --> 00:00:24.979
this is not any company's software.

00:00:25.602 --> 00:00:32.876
Bookings arrive as events. An advance purchase booking asks
for the full amount within twenty four hours. A flexible

00:00:32.876 --> 00:00:40.463
booking, thirty days out, asks for a twenty percent deposit,
seven days before arrival. When the booking changes, the old

00:00:40.463 --> 00:00:46.671
request is superseded by a new one. The rules live in one
small class that a product lead can read.

00:00:47.328 --> 00:00:55.766
Next, the card. The number is tokenised the moment it arrives.
The database keeps only the ciphertext, a data key wrapped by

00:00:55.766 --> 00:01:03.795
a master key, and the first six and last four digits. In
production the master key lives in AWS KMS, with the token as

00:01:03.795 --> 00:01:07.130
the encryption context. No card number is stored.

00:01:07.742 --> 00:01:15.655
Now the guest pays the advance purchase request, and double
clicks. The card is charged once, the payment is posted to the

00:01:15.655 --> 00:01:23.761
folio once, and the counters, read from the fake processor and
the fake PMS, say exactly that. The second click used the same

00:01:23.761 --> 00:01:26.550
idempotency key, so nothing happened twice.

00:01:27.192 --> 00:01:35.390
Real networks fail in the worst way: the processor takes the
money, and the response is lost. I arm that fault, and pay the

00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:43.521
flexible deposit. The service retries with the same key, the
processor returns its original result, and the captured total

00:01:43.521 --> 00:01:45.987
rises by the deposit once, not twice.

00:01:46.658 --> 00:01:54.416
Now the PMS goes down. A late booking is paid. The card is
charged, but the posting cannot be made. The tool does not

00:01:54.416 --> 00:02:02.373
hide it: the charge is captured, the posting waits in a dead
letter state with a reason. When the PMS is back, one click

00:02:02.373 --> 00:02:05.158
replays it, and it is posted exactly once.

00:02:05.776 --> 00:02:12.967
The operator log shows all of this with the card number
masked. A mask catches mistakes, but the code never logs the

00:02:12.967 --> 00:02:20.345
number in the first place, and the tests fail the build if a
card number ever appears in a log line or in the database.

00:02:21.047 --> 00:02:28.851
At the end of the day, finance reconciles. A clean acquirer
statement matches every capture. This messy one has a capture

00:02:28.851 --> 00:02:36.654
missing, an amount short by one dollar fifty, and a line we
never captured. Each exception has a name that a finance team

00:02:36.654 --> 00:02:37.364
can act on.

00:02:38.028 --> 00:02:45.122
Finally, shadow mode, which is how a rebuild is made safe. The
same two hundred bookings run through the new service, and

00:02:45.122 --> 00:02:52.392
each result is compared with what the legacy system does. With
the natural new rules, many bookings differ: tax rounded once

00:02:52.392 --> 00:02:59.486
on the total instead of every night, and the payment dated in
UTC instead of the hotel's day. Those are decisions for the

00:02:59.486 --> 00:03:04.586
product team, not bugs by default. With legacy compatible
rules, all two hundred match.

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:14.057
That is the slice: idempotent charges, lost responses, outages
and replays, tokenisation with envelope encryption, masked

00:03:14.057 --> 00:03:22.069
logs, reconciliation, and shadow mode, with over one hundred
and seventy automated tests behind it. The AWS key service is

00:03:22.069 --> 00:03:30.081
tested against a stub, not real AWS, and every external system
here is a fake. I built it by directing Claude Code agents,

00:03:30.081 --> 00:03:37.962
reviewing and testing what they wrote, and having two other
models review it independently for security and idempotency.

00:03:37.962 --> 00:03:45.974
The log of what the agents got wrong, and the review findings
I fixed, is in the repository. I would be glad to talk about

00:03:45.974 --> 00:03:47.550
your first two services.
